Z||000025||Wes Janson||07-14-2000||07:58 PM||||Alright then. A lot of you support me with the possibility of modifying the Fiero to support Gull Wing doords. Yes, it will include complete modification to the roof, and the doors of course.<P>But my question to all of you supporters out there is: Do any of you have workable plans or ideas? I'm still looking at pictures and blueprints to figure out ways to make it work. But the reason I came to this forum was to gather ideas and comments from others.<P>So . . . If you got an idea, help me out. I don't care how outragous or "cost effective" it is. I'm looking at all options - but for Gull Wing doors. I'm not doing any Lambo styles.<P>Let's see what you've got.<P>- Janson||||reg||1
Z||000026||William||12-26-2000||11:08 PM||||Hmmmmm. I don't recall seeing any pics of the completed gull wing doors????  HEHE||||reg||1
Z||000027||fiero56||12-27-2000||12:05 AM||||<B>"am i the only one having doubts about this gullwing door project?? how can you do this with NO DOOR FRAME??? "</B><P>Might as well save the time and frustration of the door thing and send the car to a crusher, it'll be there before the door thing works properly. Why hasn't it been done before...||||reg||1
Z||000028||JohnnyK||12-27-2000||12:08 AM||||FOr whoever said that you cannot get out of your door in a tight spot with gullwing doors, just the opposite is true. If it is hinged in the middle (DeLorean) the car would require less than a foot. Much less than the average car. However, if it is hinged on the side of the roof, as you guys are talking about, it would take about 3 feet, and look extremely stupid.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000029||onfire||12-27-2000||12:08 AM||||Just searching the web for and found this interesting site. <A HREF="http://www.spiritone.com/~cherylk/Gullwings/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.spiritone.com/~cherylk/Gullwings/</A> <P>p.s. Check out the Daewoo on the above website you could do something like that.<P><BR>------------------<BR>"I seen it on the internet, and I believe everthing on the net."<BR><A HREF="http://homestead.deja.com/user.on_fire/on_fire.html" TARGET=_blank>Check out my waist of banbwidth.</A><p>[This message has been edited by onfire (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000030||Songman||12-27-2000||11:54 AM||||I'll just have to put my two cents worth in here, or in my case it may be about a cent and a half...<P>Gull wing doors were cool in the 50's on the Mercedes, and they were cool on several cars in the 80s.. But they are old news. I'm not impressed anymore.<P>And what a pain in the @ss they are! We built a Bradley GT kit car back in the 80s and for that time it looked cool. The gull wings were more in the way than anything. At 6'2", I don't want to have to duck when I am getting in and out of my car. It is hard enough for me to fold myself in there already.<P>I liked the idea someone had about suicide doors. That would impress me! Every car I have ever seen suicide doors on from a 32 Ford roadster to an 84 Lincoln Town Car, the doors made it look just a little bit cooler.<P>If you do it, good luck... But personally, I think you are just asking for added troubles in your car and the 'cool factor' will probably not be as much as you expected.<P>OK... maybe that was about 3/4 of a cent....<p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 12-27-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||DaRkLoRD||12-29-2000||04:00 AM||||I want to try this, partially because I think it's cool looking, but also because so many people have said it can't be done, that it'd be an interesting challenge.<P>I sincerely think it will be possible to do this, and the hardest part will be making the new doors.. but even that shouldn't be any more difficult than making doors for a kit (except kits that just have new panels for the donor's doors...)<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||3
Z||000032||Phaeton||12-31-2000||04:45 PM||||A servo at the latch to pull the door tight and latch it plus a hydraulic cylinder instead of gas struts at the hinge would enable fully remote gull wing doors.<BR>All thats needed are those custom made doors.<P>This doesn't sound impossible, it just involves major hours of painfully exact precision work.||||reg||12
